<rml>
<head>
	<title>@spritesheet spike</title>
	<!--
		VaCuus-dqs.4, STEP ONE: does @spritesheet survive the VaCuus recorder AT ALL?

		Nothing in this plugin or in the 2d6 demo has ever used one, so the whole atlas
		argument rested on the vendored parser accepting the at-rule (StyleSheetParser.cpp:798)
		and on the assumption that a sprite-backed decorator records like any other textured
		draw. This document is that assumption, made falsifiable.

		ANSWERED, 2026-08-13, and the answer is yes on both counts. Headless -game run,
		vacuus.SpriteSpike + vacuus.TextureStats: NO warnings of any kind, and ONE texture
		resident at 16,384 bytes -- which is 64x64x4 exactly, i.e. a single copy of avatar.png
		feeding all four sprite decorators AND the whole-file control. The screenshot shows the
		four correct quadrants. The atlas plan in VaCuus-dqs.4 rests on a mechanism that works.

		ONE PATH TRAP ON THE WAY, worth keeping because it cost a run: a spritesheet's `src` is
		resolved DOCUMENT-RELATIVE like every other RmlUi path, so this file living under
		Tests/ made it probe Tests/img/avatar.png and fail with "Could not load texture". That
		is why it sits at the DevUI root beside every other demo document.

		WHAT IT PROVES IF IT DRAWS: the four cells below are four DIFFERENT sub-rectangles of
		ONE file, addressed by name, painted through DecoratorTiled's sprite branch
		(DecoratorTiled.cpp:284-287) rather than through <img src>. Four sprites, one texture,
		one LoadTexture -- which is the entire mechanism the catalogue in VaCuus-dqs.4 needs.

		WHAT IT DOES NOT PROVE: anything about memory savings. avatar.png is one 64x64 file, so
		the loose spelling of this same document would ALSO be one texture -- RmlUi dedups by
		SOURCE (TextureDatabase.cpp:93-104), so N elements sharing an src already cost one.
		The saving an atlas buys is over N DIFFERENT files, and measuring that needs the
		catalogue's own art.

		NO TEXT ANYWHERE, deliberately: a font atlas is a texture too (through GenerateTexture,
		the callback database), and the point of this run is a clean texture count.
	-->
	<style>
		body { width: 100%; height: 100%; background-color: #101014; }

		/*
			The at-rule. `src` is a plain string and each remaining key is an arbitrary sprite
			name whose value is `x y w h` (SpritesheetPropertyParser, StyleSheetParser.cpp:146-191).
			avatar.png is 64x64, so this cuts it into four 32x32 quadrants -- the cheapest
			possible sheet, using art the plugin already ships.
		*/
		@spritesheet spike-sheet
		{
			src: img/avatar.png;

			spike-tl: 0px 0px 32px 32px;
			spike-tr: 32px 0px 32px 32px;
			spike-bl: 0px 32px 32px 32px;
			spike-br: 32px 32px 32px 32px;
		}

		.cell {
			display: block;
			position: absolute;
			width: 96px;
			height: 96px;
			border: 1px #3a3a38;
		}

		/* THE CLAIM UNDER TEST, four times: a decorator naming a SPRITE rather than a file. */
		#tl { left: 40px;  top: 40px;  decorator: image(spike-tl); }
		#tr { left: 160px; top: 40px;  decorator: image(spike-tr); }
		#bl { left: 40px;  top: 160px; decorator: image(spike-bl); }
		#br { left: 160px; top: 160px; decorator: image(spike-br); }

		/* THE CONTROL, and it is what makes the shot readable: the same file drawn WHOLE
		   through the ordinary image decorator. If the four cells above are blank and this one
		   is not, the sprite path is what failed rather than the texture load. */
		#whole { left: 300px; top: 40px; width: 128px; height: 128px; decorator: image(img/avatar.png); }
	</style>
</head>
<body>
	<div class="cell" id="tl"/>
	<div class="cell" id="tr"/>
	<div class="cell" id="bl"/>
	<div class="cell" id="br"/>
	<div class="cell" id="whole"/>
</body>
</rml>
